@@ -837,15 +837,41 @@ Manage records without starting a model, from the project directory:
 
 ```sh
 kiln memory export                 # JSON, including correction history
+kiln memory import backup.json     # ingest an export into this project
 kiln memory forget test-runner     # deletes every version of this key
 kiln memory restore 12             # restores this project's version as a new record
 ```
 
+`import` reads exactly one JSON array in the export format and ingests it into
+the current project scope (or the global preferences scope with
+`kiln memory global import`). It is a local command and never calls a model. For
+each key it writes only the largest-input-id record as the key's current
+version; the exported history itself is not replayed. Imported records land in
+the destination scope with fresh ids and creation times, their expiry preserved.
+Project source bindings are rebound from their original root-relative path into
+this project's root and re-hashed against the destination file; missing,
+changed, or escaping sources are rejected. Global import accepts preferences
+without sources only.
+
+`--on-conflict` chooses what happens when a key already has an active record in
+the destination: `error` (the default) rejects the whole batch, `skip` leaves
+existing records untouched, and `replace` supersedes them while preserving local
+correction history. `--dry-run` runs the same validation and conflict analysis
+but writes nothing. On success the command prints one JSON object with integer
+`inserted`, `replaced`, and `skipped` counts (per current key, not historical
+rows) and a boolean `dry_run`; dry-run counts describe what would happen:
+
+```sh
+kiln memory import backup.json --dry-run --on-conflict replace
+# {"inserted":2,"replaced":1,"skipped":0,"dry_run":true}
+```
+
 Explicit user preferences can be shared across projects:
 
 ```sh
 kiln memory global remember response-style "Prefer concise answers"
 kiln memory global export
+kiln memory global import prefs.json
 kiln memory global forget response-style
 kiln memory global restore 12
 ```
